(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz-version-6"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 6)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many deciduous ('milk') teeth erupt between about 6 and 30 months after birth?","choices":["16","32","24","20"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are 20 deciduous teeth, which erupt between roughly 6 and 30 months of age before eventually being lost and replaced. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"The 20 deciduous teeth are eventually lost and replaced by how many permanent teeth?","choices":["32","28","20","36"],"answer":0,"exp":"The permanent dentition consists of 32 teeth, replacing the 20 deciduous teeth and adding additional posterior teeth including the molars. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which teeth are the last to erupt, often in the late teens or early 20s, and may become impacted if the jaw lacks space?","choices":["The central incisor teeth","The permanent canine teeth","Third molars (wisdom teeth)","The second premolar teeth"],"answer":2,"exp":"The third molars ('wisdom teeth') are the last teeth to erupt and are the most commonly impacted when jaw space is insufficient. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An impacted tooth is one that:","choices":["Lacks a complete enamel covering over the crown","Erupts significantly earlier than the normal eruption schedule","Cannot erupt properly because of the angle of its growth","Has an unusually short root and stunted development"],"answer":2,"exp":"Impacted teeth cannot erupt properly because of the angle at which they are growing, often emerging only partially or not at all. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of total saliva do the parotid glands, the largest salivary glands, produce?","choices":["60–70%","90–95%","25–30%","3–5%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The parotid glands, though the largest salivary glands, contribute about 25–30% of total saliva. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The sublingual glands, located inferior to the tongue, contribute approximately what share of total saliva?","choices":["50%","3–5%","60–70%","25–30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sublingual glands contribute only about 3–5% of total saliva, the smallest share of the three paired salivary glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"The parotid gland has a close anatomical relationship with which nerve as it courses through the gland?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The parotid gland has a close relationship with the facial nerve, which passes through the substance of the gland. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The parotid duct, which conveys saliva from the parotid gland to the oral cavity, is also known by which eponym?","choices":["Stensen's duct","Wharton's duct","Bartholin's duct","Rivinus' duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"The parotid duct opens into the oral cavity by way of Stensen's duct. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The submandibular duct opens into the floor of the mouth through a papilla located:","choices":["Directly beneath the hard palate","Posterior to the sublingual ducts","Lateral to the lingual frenulum","Within the vestibule of the mouth"],"answer":2,"exp":"The submandibular duct opens through a papilla in the floor of the mouth, lateral to the lingual frenulum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"The dorsal (superior) surface of the tongue is covered with numerous small projections called:","choices":["Rugae","Papillae","Haustra","Plicae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Numerous small projections called papillae cover the superior (dorsal) surface of the tongue. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Lymphoid tissue on the posterior surface of the tongue is known as the:","choices":["Palatine tonsils","Tubal tonsils","Pharyngeal tonsil","Lingual tonsils"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior surface of the tongue contains the lingual tonsils. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The esophagus begins at approximately which cervical vertebral level, corresponding to the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":["C2","C8","C6","C4"],"answer":2,"exp":"The esophagus begins at approximately the C6 level, where the upper esophageal sphincter is located. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at approximately which vertebral level?","choices":["T8","T10","T12","T6"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at approximately the T10 level. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The esophagus terminates at approximately which vertebral level, where it meets the stomach at the cardiac sphincter?","choices":["T8","T10","T9","T11"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophagus terminates at approximately the T11 level, at the cardiac (inferior esophageal) sphincter. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The three named portions of the esophagus, in order from superior to inferior, are the:","choices":["Cervical, mediastinal, abdominal","Cervical, thoracic, pelvic","Pharyngeal, cervical, abdominal","Cervical, diaphragmatic, abdominal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophagus is divided into cervical, mediastinal, and abdominal portions as it descends toward the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The cricopharyngeal muscle forms which sphincter of the esophagus?","choices":["The inferior (cardiac) sphincter","The pyloric sphincter of the stomach","The ileocecal valve sphincter","The superior esophageal sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior esophageal sphincter, located where the pharynx and esophagus meet, is formed by the cricopharyngeal muscle. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The adult esophagus measures approximately how long as it travels from the pharynx to the stomach?","choices":["15 cm","25 cm","10 cm","40 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophagus is approximately 25 cm long in the adult, extending from the pharynx to the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"The submucosa of the esophagus is described as containing thick elastic fibers and which glandular structures?","choices":["Gastric pits","Crypts of Lieberkühn","Brunner's glands","Mucous glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophageal submucosa contains thick elastic fibers and mucous glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which stomach region is the most superior and lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","choices":["Pyloric antrum","Cardia","Fundus","Body"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fundus is the most superior region of the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which mucosal specialization of the stomach interior consists of a narrow channel along the lesser curvature that helps direct fluids toward the pylorus?","choices":["Rugae","Plicae circulares","Gastric pits","Gastric canal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gastric canal is the mucosal specialization of the stomach interior described alongside rugae, pits, and glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Ingested nutrients typically spend at least how long in the small intestine, according to the lecture?","choices":["2 hours","6 hours","24 hours","12 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture states that ingested nutrients spend at least 12 hours in the small intestine. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"The small intestine is described as a thin-walled, coiled tube of approximately what length?","choices":["6 meters (20 feet)","3 meters (10 feet)","9 meters (30 feet)","1.5 meters (5 feet)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The small intestine is described as approximately 6 meters (20 feet) long. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"The jejunum makes up approximately what fraction of the small intestine's total length?","choices":["One-fifth","Two-fifths","Three-fifths","Four-fifths"],"answer":1,"exp":"The jejunum makes up approximately two-fifths of the small intestine's total length. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"The ileum makes up approximately what fraction of the small intestine's total length?","choices":["Two-fifths","All but the duodenum","Three-fifths","One-fifth"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ileum forms approximately three-fifths of the small intestine's total length, the largest share of the three segments. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"The four parts of the duodenum, in order, are the superior, descending, transverse, and:","choices":["Retroperitoneal part","Terminal part","Distal part","Ascending part"],"answer":3,"exp":"The four parts of the duodenum are the superior, descending, transverse, and ascending parts. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which part of the duodenum is described as the longest?","choices":["Superior part","Transverse part","Ascending part","Descending part"],"answer":1,"exp":"The transverse part of the duodenum is described as the longest of the four duodenal segments. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which part of the duodenum is intraperitoneal, unlike the rest of the largely retroperitoneal duodenum?","choices":["The ascending part near the duodenojejunal flexure","The superior part (duodenal cap/ampulla)","The descending part next to the pancreatic head","The transverse part crossing the aorta"],"answer":1,"exp":"The first (superior) part of the duodenum, also called the duodenal cap or ampulla, is intraperitoneal, while the remainder of the duodenum is retroperitoneal. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The junction where the ascending part of the duodenum turns to become the jejunum, marked by a suspensory ligament, is called the:","choices":["Duodenojejunal flexure","Hepatic colic flexure","Splenic colic flexure","Ileocecal junction"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ascending part of the duodenum ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, where it becomes the jejunum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The suspensory structure at the duodenojejunal flexure that helps anchor this junction is known by which eponym?","choices":["Ligament of Treitz","Ligamentum teres","Falciform ligament","Gastrosplenic ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ligament of Treitz is the suspensory structure at the duodenojejunal flexure, marking the retroperitoneal-to-intraperitoneal transition of the small intestine. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"The jejunum is located predominantly in which abdominal region?","choices":["Left lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Left upper quadrant"],"answer":3,"exp":"The jejunum lies mostly in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
